    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107641,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 278,
        "headingId": 467,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T14:17:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 705,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 468,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107642,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 278,
        "headingId": 468,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>GA15.Y Other specified acquired abnormalities of cervix uteri</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T14:17:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 706,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 469,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107643,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 278,
        "headingId": 469,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T14:17:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 707,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 470,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107644,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 278,
        "headingId": 470,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T14:17:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 708,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 471,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107645,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 278,
        "headingId": 471,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Mesonephric remnants and hyperplasia are typically localized in the lateral uterine cervical wall, but they may be found anywhere along the lateral aspects of the urogenital tract.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T14:17:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 709,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 472,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107646,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 278,
        "headingId": 472,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Mesonephric remnants and hyperplasia are usually an incidental finding. Hyperplasia may rarely manifest with abnormal cervical cytology or as a mass or expansion of the cervical wall.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T14:17:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 710,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 473,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107647,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 278,
        "headingId": 473,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Mesonephric remnants and hyperplasia are detected in as many as 20% of cervixes.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T14:17:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 711,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 474,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107648,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 278,
        "headingId": 474,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T14:17:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 712,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 475,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107649,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 278,
        "headingId": 475,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Mesonephric remnants and hyperplasia are thought to arise from residual (non-regressed) cells of the mesonephric (Wolffian) duct.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T14:17:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 719,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 482,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107650,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 278,
        "headingId": 482,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Mesonephric remnants are generally an incidental microscopic finding. Rarely, mesonephric hyperplasia may form a mass.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T14:17:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 713,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 476,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107651,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 278,
        "headingId": 476,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Remnants are composed of clusters or linear arrays of small to medium-sized tubules lined by cuboidal cells with scant eosinophilic cytoplasm and round to ovoid nuclei with fine chromatin. Dense eosinophilic PAS-positive intraluminal secretions are usually present. Mitoses are inconspicuous. Hyperplasia is a more robust proliferation of mesonephric tubules with histological features similar to those of remnants. It may be of lobular (most common), diffuse, ductal, or mixed type {2252101; 29269124}. Diffuse hyperplasia lacks lobular growth and has tubules that are architecturally simple and separated by stroma. Ductal hyperplasia shows proliferation of ductal structures, which often have clefts; it may be associated with peripheral tubules. Although not necessary for diagnosis, CD10 (luminal/apical), calretinin, GATA3, thyroid transcription factor&nbsp;1 (TTF1), and PAX8 are frequently positive {26135559; 25851711; 22456609}, and ER, PR, and p16 are negative {25272301}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T14:17:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 714,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 477,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107652,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 278,
        "headingId": 477,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Mesonephric remnants rarely extend to the cervical canal, showing cuboidal, hyperchromatic cells without pseudostratification or mitoses in cervical cytology sampling {16181310}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T14:17:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 715,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 478,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107653,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 278,
        "headingId": 478,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T14:17:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 718,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 481,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107654,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 278,
        "headingId": 481,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> small to medium-sized tubules lined by cuboidal cells with scant eosinophilic cytoplasm and round to ovoid bland nuclei; location in the lateral walls of the cervix, vagina, or uterus.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T14:17:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 716,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 479,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107655,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 278,
        "headingId": 479,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not applicable</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T14:17:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 717,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 480,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107656,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 278,
        "headingId": 480,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Mesonephric remnants and hyperplasia are benign. Occasionally, renal abnormalities, including ipsilateral renal dysgenesis or ureterocoele, occur in association with mesonephric remnants or tubulocystic anomalies of the mesonephric duct {30446299}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T14:17:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]